+In the failed insertion shown in Chapter~6, the first camera frame carried the same weight as the last. The attempt lasted 15.233 seconds. From that recording, the visual model took 16 frames and averaged their features into a single description of the action. Reading the frames in reverse order would have given exactly the same description. For an insertion, this matters. A change near the point of contact may be visible in only a few frames, and averaging over the whole action can reduce its influence.
+
+The robot also recorded force, torque, and movement during the insertion. These measurements offered a second view of the same event. The main REASSEMBLE experiment first made a prediction from the camera and another from the sensors. It then tested whether combining the two helped identify failed actions. The recorded failure label made that comparison possible, even though the physical cause of this particular failure was unknown.
+
+PHM raised a different question. There, the aim was to predict a numerical indicator of gear condition from machine measurements. Photographs taken after a run supplied the target for the main sensor study. A photograph used to define what a model should predict has a different role from a camera frame supplied to a model when it makes a prediction. That distinction affects what can be claimed from the fusion experiments in this chapter.
+
+\section{What the models predicted}
+
+In REASSEMBLE, each completed action had one recorded outcome: success or failure. The 16 frames from the insertion therefore belonged to \textbf{one prediction}. Treating them as 16 separate test cases would count the same outcome 16 times. A recording could also contain several actions carried out under similar conditions. For this reason, the evaluation kept actions from the same recording together when separating training and assessment data.
+
+Failure was the positive outcome in REASSEMBLE, ViFailback, and HANDOVER. In MSM, the positive outcome was an abnormal cutting interval under the label definition used in that study. PHM used regression because its target was a number. A low error in PHM meant that a prediction was close to the indicator derived from the photographs. Establishing how closely that indicator measured actual tooth damage would require an independent physical measurement.
+
+For the REASSEMBLE insertion, let \(p_v\) be the failure probability estimated from video and \(p_s\) the probability estimated from sensors. Both estimates refer to the same action. Their values can differ because the models receive different measurements and learn different relationships from the training data.
+
+A classification model produces a score. A sigmoid function turns that score into a value between zero and one. Whether the value is a good probability estimate is a separate issue. For example, if a model gives many actions a failure probability close to 0.8, about eight in ten of those actions should fail if the model is well calibrated in that range. This chapter distinguishes models that combine probabilities directly from models that first convert them to \textit{log-odds}, using \(\ell(p)=\log[p/(1-p)]\). The software clips probabilities near zero and one so this calculation remains defined.
+
+\section{What the camera contributed}
+
+The REASSEMBLE visual model began with a detector trained to find objects in images. RT-DETR has a convolutional backbone that extracts image features at several scales. Its hybrid encoder combines information across those scales. A decoder and detection heads then use the features to predict object classes and bounding boxes \parencite{Zhao2024RTDETR}. For the insertion study, the available label described whether the action succeeded. There was no target box marking the place where it failed.
+
+The main experiment used the ResNet18-vd backbone from the \texttt{PekingU/rtdetr\_r18vd} checkpoint. Its weights stayed fixed. Each hand-camera frame was resized to \(640 \times 640\) pixels, and its pixel values were divided by 255. The recorded procedure used no further scaling with the ImageNet mean and standard deviation.
+
+For each frame, the backbone returned three feature maps. A feature map records how strongly an image feature responds at different positions. The model took the average response across each map channel and joined the results. This gave 896 numbers per frame. Averaging those numbers across the 16 selected frames produced 896 numbers for the completed action. A separately trained classification head used them to estimate the probability of failure. The detector’s hybrid encoder, decoder, and bounding-box heads were bypassed in this experiment.
+
+Two details of the insertion disappear during those averages. The first is the exact position of a feature in the image. The second is when that feature appeared. For example, one feature might respond strongly in a frame near contact and weakly in the other frames. Its average could be the same as a moderate response spread across several frames. The resulting 896 values would not tell the prediction head which pattern had occurred.
+
+This limitation led to a specific comparison in Chapter~6. The main model averaged frames across the whole action. A later experiment pooled features from the last four of the sixteen sampled frames, giving the final part of the movement its own representation. Both models answered a question about a completed action. Testing an early warning would require an earlier decision time and would exclude every frame recorded after it.
+
+Previous studies give reasons to compare these choices. Pretrained convolutional features have been useful in industrial inspection \parencite{Roth2022Towards}, and the Vision Transformer study showed that the amount of pretraining data affects the comparison between convolution and attention \parencite{P010}. Video Swin keeps local information across both space and time, with a computing cost that depends on the clips and image crops used \parencite{P013}. TimeSformer reported an advantage for separate spatial and temporal attention on Kinetics-400 and Something-Something V2 \parencite[Table~1]{P034}. The question for REASSEMBLE was narrower: with the available recordings and training time, did retaining a selected part of the action help predict its outcome?
+
+PHM also used a model from the RT-DETR family, but its implementation followed a different route. The software ran a frozen RT-DETR-L detector and collected three intermediate feature maps during its prediction process. Pooling these maps produced 768 image features. The software added the time taken to extract them, so the regression head received \textbf{769 inputs}. Any interpretation of this image experiment must account for that timing value as well as the photograph.
+
+Keeping an encoder frozen also allowed its features to be saved and reused across training splits. Later experiments investigated what changed when the visual features themselves were allowed to adapt. In ViFailback, the study distinguished a generic model pretrained on COCO, a model previously trained with REASSEMBLE labels, and the completed stages of training on the target task. These models had different training histories. Comparing their epoch counts alone would miss differences in batch size, learning rate, image augmentation, and early stopping. Where the record contains only saved features, it confirms feature extraction. A claim about classification or fusion also requires a completed assessment.
+
+\textit{Implementation records: \EvidenceRef{E064}{}, \EvidenceRef{E065}{}, \EvidenceRef{E033}{}.}
+
+\section{What the machine sensors contributed}
+
+During a REASSEMBLE action, the robot recorded 22 channels: three force channels, three torque channels, two gripper channels, seven joint-effort channels, and seven joint-velocity channels. A brief change in force and a gradual rise in force can have the same average. The sensor study therefore compared two ways of reading the same measurements. One described the action with a small set of statistics. The other learned from short, overlapping parts of the signal.
+
+For the statistical model, each channel supplied ten values: the mean, standard deviation, median, minimum, maximum, lower and upper quartiles, range, root-mean-square amplitude, and slope over action progress. Together, the 22 channels produced \textbf{220 features per action}. For a force channel, the maximum retained the highest value in the resampled force channel. The range described the distance between its lowest and highest values. The slope described whether force generally rose or fell as the action progressed. None of these values, on its own, identified the moment when the highest force occurred.
+
+These simple features were a serious comparison for the learned model. They captured useful properties of the robot’s movement, and their compact size made them a practical reference with the available training data. Other industrial work has likewise shown that carefully chosen features can compete with learned representations, including in 3D anomaly detection \parencite{Horwitz2023Back}.
+
+The second sensor model used PatchTST. To prepare its input, each completed action was represented by 512 positions from start to finish on each of the 22 channels. Position 256 was therefore near the middle of the action, whether that action lasted a few seconds or much longer. The spacing was measured in \textbf{action progress}. Two neighbouring positions did not necessarily represent the same number of milliseconds in different actions. This matters if the signal is later interpreted in terms of physical vibration frequencies.
+
