-\section{Target Provenance, Prediction Units, and Available Inputs}
+\section{Targets, prediction units, and decision time}
 \label{sec:ch4-targets}
 
-A scientifically interpretable prediction task requires an explicit definition of what is predicted, how its reference value is obtained, and which observations are available at the intended prediction time. A model’s predictive performance alone cannot establish that its target accurately represents the underlying physical or operational phenomenon. This distinction is particularly relevant to the PHM investigation. Its image-derived candidate-area measure represents a provisional gear-condition proxy. Predicting the proxy from machine-sensor observations measures agreement with the constructed target. It does not independently establish the accuracy of physical wear estimation, tooth-damage measurement, or remaining-useful-life prediction.
+For PHM, the principal prediction unit was a gear run. An image-processing rule applied to post-run photographs measured the area that met the rule’s selection criteria. This value served as a \emph{proxy}: a quantity available for study when a direct measurement of the condition of interest was unavailable. The main sensor model tried to predict this photographic value from machine measurements. Its error describes agreement with that target. Claims about actual damage, damage to a particular tooth, or remaining useful life would need their own physical reference.
 
-In PHM, the principal prediction unit is a gear experiment run, and the target is a photograph-derived continuous condition proxy. REASSEMBLE predicts the recorded success or failure of completed robotic assembly actions. MSM predicts abnormal cutting within milling observation intervals. ViFailback predicts the recorded success or failure of complete manipulation episodes, while HANDOVER investigates binary outcomes associated with human–robot interaction trials. The MSM investigation retains its declared mapping of native milling labels. Alternative mappings are considered through sensitivity analyses because changing the interpretation of a native label also changes the prediction task.
+The robotic studies had recorded outcomes. REASSEMBLE predicted whether a completed assembly action had succeeded. ViFailback predicted the outcome of a complete manipulation episode. HANDOVER used a binary outcome for a human–robot trial. In MSM, the target was abnormal cutting during a labelled milling interval. The main MSM analysis used its stated mapping of the original labels. Other mappings were examined separately because changing the meaning of “abnormal” also changes the task.
 
-Sampling visual frames across a complete robotic action can provide information about its concluding state. Similarly, sensor statistics calculated over an entire milling interval describe observations collected throughout that interval. These representations support retrospective assessment of condition or outcome. On their own, they do not show that a model can make a useful prediction before the operation is complete. Prospective monitoring would require a defined decision time, causal preprocessing, a specified lead-time measure, and target annotations appropriate to the intended prediction horizon.
+The end of the observation window matters in each case. A hand-camera frame taken near the end of an insertion can show part of the outcome. A sensor average over a whole milling interval includes the final measurements from that interval. Both can be useful for reviewing a completed event. To test an early warning, the study would need an earlier decision time, a rule allowing only data already available at that time, and a measure of how much warning the prediction gave. A short model runtime cannot recover time already spent waiting for the observation window to finish.
 
-The distinction also influences computational interpretation. A model may produce a prediction rapidly after receiving its input, while acquiring the required observation window introduces additional delay. The total time required for an operational prediction therefore includes both observation acquisition and the subsequent processing and inference stages.
+Some inputs also carry information about the experiment itself. In the separate PHM image-regression experiment, extraction time was one of the inputs alongside the image features. In REASSEMBLE, action type carried information because insertion and placement had different failure rates. A gate that changes its weights between these actions may have learned a difference in task difficulty. To claim that it detected a damaged sensor, its behaviour must also be tested under a known change in sensor quality.
 
-Process identity, observation duration, time index, and other contextual variables can contain predictive information independently of the principal physical observations. For example, the extraction latency included in the PHM visual regression representation introduces a timing-related covariate alongside image-derived information. Similarly, an action-conditioned fusion mechanism may exploit differences in failure prevalence between action categories. Its predictive improvement would not automatically establish that it recognised a deterioration in physical sensor quality. Context-only references and constant-adjustment controls provide ways to investigate these alternative explanations where the corresponding comparisons are available.
-
-\section{Grouped Assessment, Data Splits, and Leakage Prevention}
+\section{Keeping development and assessment data separate}
 \label{sec:ch4-assessment}
 
-Industrial and robotic observations often exhibit dependence because several samples originate from the same physical experiment, recording, task, machine, participant, or acquisition session. Randomly partitioning individual observations may therefore place closely related samples into both development and assessment populations. Grouped assessment prevents overlap at the specified dependence-group level. It does not, by itself, establish independence at higher physical or operational levels.
+The correct group to hold out depended on how the observations were collected. PHM held out complete gear experiments. REASSEMBLE kept all actions from one recording together. ViFailback held out manipulation tasks. MSM examined both experiment-based and date-based groups. A milling machine or tool could appear on more than one date, so a test on a held-out date answers a narrower question than a test in a new factory.
 
-The principal PHM sensor protocol holds out complete gear experiments, while REASSEMBLE groups actions by recording. MSM examines experiment-based and acquisition-date groupings, and ViFailback separates manipulation tasks. HANDOVER retains the published official trial partitions. The HANDOVER dataset publication describes its official participant-disjoint assessment. However, the available participant-to-trial correspondence does not permit independent reconstruction of that grouping or the construction of participant-disjoint inner training folds. For MSM, an acquisition date may include multiple tools or operators. Physical equipment may also recur across dates. Consequently, date-disjoint assessment does not establish generalisation to an entirely new machine, factory, or installation.
+HANDOVER used the published official partitions. The dataset publication describes the test participants as separate from the training participants. The available participant-to-trial records were insufficient to reconstruct that separation independently or to make new participant-level folds within training. This is why the official comparison remained usable while the planned learned stacker had to be left out.
 
-\subsection{Training-Side Preprocessing and Model Selection}
+\subsection{Preprocessing and model selection}
 
-Within each outer assessment split, all data-dependent modelling operations must be considered part of model development. These operations include feature scaling and normalisation, imputation parameters estimated from data, feature selection, supervised representation learning, hyperparameter selection, probability calibration, decision-threshold selection, and learned fusion fitting.
+Information can enter a model before the final training step. Consider sensor scaling. A model may express each reading as a distance from the average training value. If that average includes the held-out experiment, the test measurements have already influenced the values given to the model. The same issue applies when filling gaps, selecting features, tuning a model, adjusting probabilities, or choosing a decision threshold. These steps need to use the development data allowed for that split \parencite{P017}. Reusing evaluation results to select a configuration can make its reported performance look too favourable \parencite{P004}.
 
-Using cross-validation to select model configurations can produce optimistic error estimates if the same evaluation evidence is later used to report the selected performance \parencite{P004}. More generally, leakage must be assessed through the complete modelling procedure, not only through the final training operation \parencite{P017}. When a representation is derived using a fixed, generic pretrained encoder without target-specific supervision, its features may be cached before target-specific model fitting. However, any data-dependent normalisation, supervised adaptation, or feature selection must respect the corresponding permitted development population.
+The PHM records contain a concrete example. The principal sensor experiment fitted its normaliser again inside each training fold. A separate four-model diagnostic fitted the normaliser before the program changed which gear experiment was held out. In that diagnostic, measurements from the held-out experiment could affect scaling. The two procedures are reported separately. Measuring how much this changed the diagnostic result would require running it again with scaling fitted inside each fold.
 
-The principal PHM sensor protocol fits its normaliser separately within each training fold. However, a separate four-arm diagnostic runner fits its normaliser before entering the loop that changes the held-out gear experiment. In that diagnostic, observations from the held-out experiments therefore influence the fitted preprocessing transformation. The principal PHM experiment and the four-arm diagnostic must consequently remain separate when reporting their procedures and interpreting their results. A fully fold-isolated estimate for the four-arm diagnostic would require repeating the corresponding experiment under a revised preprocessing procedure. Such a repetition is not part of the completed evidence.
+A frozen image encoder presents a different case. If its weights were learned from an external dataset and its feature extraction uses no target labels or statistics from the assessment population, the fixed image features can be saved in advance. Later steps that \emph{learn} from those features still have to respect the training split.
 
-\subsection{Out-of-Fold Predictions and Learned Fusion}
+\subsection{Training the REASSEMBLE stacker}
 
-Prediction-level stacking requires a second-stage training population containing the outputs of its first-stage predictors. Using predictions generated from observations already included in first-stage fitting can provide the stacker with an unrealistically favourable description of branch performance. Stacked generalisation therefore motivates constructing second-stage training inputs through cross-validated first-stage predictions \parencite{Ting1999Issues}.
+The REASSEMBLE stacker learned from two numbers for each training action: a camera score and a sensor score. It needed scores like the ones it would receive for an unfamiliar recording. A score from a branch that had already trained on the same recording could give the stacker an unrealistically easy example.
 
-For each outer assessment split, the outer training population is partitioned into grouped inner fitting and prediction subsets. Branch models generate predictions for observations whose corresponding recordings were excluded from their fitting. These predictions form an out-of-fold prediction bank used by subsequent training-side calibration, model-selection, and fusion-fitting procedures according to the relevant protocol. The final selected branch models and prediction-level combination are then used to generate predictions for the designated outer assessment recordings.
+The study first set aside the recordings for the outer assessment. Within the remaining recordings, the camera and sensor models were fitted on some groups and used to predict actions from other groups. Repeating this process produced a bank of \emph{out-of-fold predictions}. For each row in the bank, the model that produced the prediction had excluded that row’s recording. These scores supported training-side calibration, model selection, and fusion fitting according to the procedure being tested \parencite{Ting1999Issues}.
 
-Within each outer split, the stacker's internal validation reuses an out-of-fold prediction bank generated from the outer training recordings. Although each predicted row originates from a branch that excluded its own recording, a branch used to generate a prediction for a stacker training row may have been fitted on recordings subsequently assigned to a stacker validation subset. The outer assessment recordings remain excluded from fitting. The shared prediction bank therefore limits claims of strict independence at the deeper meta-validation stage without, by itself, establishing leakage from the outer assessment population.
+One detail limits how independently the stacker itself was validated. Its internal validation used the same prediction bank. A branch that produced a score for a stacker-training row could have learned from a recording later used to validate the stacker. This dependence occurred \textbf{inside the outer training population}. The outer assessment recordings stayed outside fitting and entered only when the selected system was scored.
 
