-\section{Model Fitting, Selection, Calibration, and Threshold Procedures}
+\section{Fitting models, adjusting probabilities, and choosing thresholds}
 \label{sec:ch4-fitting}
 
-Each empirical comparison identifies its trainable components, initialisation procedure, preprocessing, candidate configurations, training budget, and relevant stopping or selection conditions. Several more extensive experiments completed only a subset of their originally planned configurations. Their interpretation is restricted to the comparisons for which the required fitting and assessment stages were completed. The prediction procedure distinguishes four operations: fitting model parameters, selecting a model, calibrating probabilities, and selecting a decision threshold.
+Fitting a model, choosing a model, adjusting its probabilities, and choosing a decision threshold were separate steps. In REASSEMBLE, for example, training the sensor predictor determined how its features contributed to its score. Selecting its regularisation setting determined which fitted version went forward. A calibrator then used training-side predictions and outcomes to adjust the probabilities. Finally, a threshold converted each probability into a success or failure decision.
 
-Model fitting estimates the parameters of the selected prediction architecture using its permitted training observations. Model selection subsequently determines which representation, hyperparameter configuration, or fitting procedure is retained for assessment. Equating the number of training epochs does not necessarily mean equivalent optimisation exposure if the configurations differ in batch size, available observations, early stopping, initialisation, or trainable components. Similarly, a pretrained frozen representation and a scratch-trained temporal encoder receive different forms of prior information and allocate computational resources differently.
+The main REASSEMBLE branch calibrators used sigmoid functions fitted to selected inner out-of-fold scores. Calibration can improve the meaning of a probability estimate, but the improvement needs to be checked on assessment data \parencite{Guo2017Calibration}. The F2 stacker then fitted a logistic combination of the two branch probabilities with its own intercept. There was no further calibration step after F2. The original context gates combined branch log-odds using weights that added up to one. They had neither a free final intercept nor a separate calibrator after the gate.
 
-Calibration methods use development-side predictions and corresponding outcomes to adjust the numerical interpretation of prediction scores. Validation-based calibration can improve probability quality under appropriate conditions, but its effectiveness must be assessed rather than assumed \parencite{Guo2017Calibration}. The principal REASSEMBLE branch calibrators use sigmoid logistic maps fitted to selected inner out-of-fold prediction scores.
+This difference matters when interpreting the gain from fusion. Suppose a sensor-only adjustment raises some probabilities while keeping all actions within one fold in the same order. The ranking within that fold stays the same. A second fold, however, has its own fitted adjustment. When the two lists are joined, a failure in the second fold can move above a success in the first. The \textbf{pooled} ranking can therefore change even though the order within each fold stays fixed.
 
-F2 introduces a fitted logistic map with a free intercept and separate branch coefficients. It does not include an additional post-stacker calibration stage in the recorded core implementation. The original context-conditioned gates combine branch logits through normalised weights and apply a sigmoid to the resulting weighted value. They do not include a free final intercept or an additional post-gate calibrator.
+In the main REASSEMBLE comparison, F2 improved pooled failure ranking over the original statistical sensor predictor. F2 received both a camera score and an extra fitted step for combining scores. A sensor-only model given the same opportunity for second-stage fitting would help separate those effects. The original comparison had no independently refitted control of that kind. Chapter~6 examines the saved predictions to see what the fitted terms contributed. The complete gain cannot be assigned to the camera alone.
 
-A strictly increasing transformation preserves the ordering of observations within a fixed assessment population. Consequently, it preserves ranking-based metrics such as average precision and AUROC within that population, subject to numerical precision and tie handling. When a sensor probability is transformed through a logistic function with a positive sensor coefficient, its ordering remains unchanged within the corresponding fold. However, independently fitted transformations can differ between assessment folds. When predictions from different folds are pooled, these transformations may change the relative ordering of observations originating from different folds, even though the ordering within each fold remains unchanged. Consequently, pooled average precision may change even when the ranking within every individual fold remains unchanged.
+For binary decisions, the recorded procedure examined thresholds from 0.01 to 0.99 using permitted training-side predictions and a stated objective, such as balanced accuracy. A score at or above the selected threshold counted as positive. This produced a reproducible decision for the experiment. A factory alarm would need additional information about the cost of a missed failure, a false alarm, or an unnecessary stop.
 
-A comparison can involve both additional information from another prediction branch and an additional fitted transformation of the existing score. Within-fold discrimination, pooled ranking, and probability calibration therefore represent distinct effects. A matched sensor-only second-stage predictor provides an additional control for investigating the contribution of the fitted transformation. Where such a separately fitted control is unavailable, arithmetic analysis of the retained model can provide narrower evidence about the contribution of particular terms without resolving the complete attribution problem. REASSEMBLE’s attribution of the full pooled fusion improvement is restricted by the absence of an independently refitted sensor-only second-stage control. The corresponding REASSEMBLE saved-prediction investigation is presented in Chapter~6.
-
-Observations with prediction scores at or above the selected threshold are classified as positive, while those below the threshold are classified as negative. The original implementation examines a threshold grid ranging from 0.01 to 0.99 using permitted training-side predictions and a specified selection objective, such as balanced accuracy. A threshold selected in this way belongs to the experimental evaluation protocol. It is not necessarily an operationally optimal industrial alarm threshold. Industrial decision-making may assign different costs to missed failures, false alarms, interruptions, or unnecessary maintenance. Establishing an operationally appropriate threshold would require information about these consequences and the intended monitoring application.
-
-\section{Metrics and Uncertainty Estimation}
+\section{Measuring performance and uncertainty}
 \label{sec:ch4-metrics}
 
-Regression error, classification discrimination, probability quality, and threshold-dependent decisions describe different properties of a prediction model. Their interpretation also depends on the target definition, class distribution, assessment population, and aggregation procedure.
+\subsection{What the reported scores mean}
 
-\subsection{Regression, Classification, and Probability Metrics}
+In PHM, \textbf{mean absolute error} gives the average size of the difference between a predicted value and the photographic target. \textbf{Root mean squared error} gives greater weight to larger errors. Both retain the target’s units. The coefficient of determination, \(R^2\), compares prediction error with the variation in the observed targets. It can become negative, and it can change sharply when a held-out group contains little target variation. Tooth-level and run-level errors were therefore reported at their own levels.
 
-Mean absolute error describes the average absolute difference between predicted and observed target values. Root mean squared error calculates the square root of the average squared prediction error. Both measures retain the units of the prediction target. The coefficient of determination compares prediction error against the variation of the observed target values. It may become negative when the fitted predictor performs poorly relative to its reference denominator and can be unstable when the variation within an assessment group is limited. Tooth-level and run-level regression measurements are reported separately because they represent different prediction units and dependence structures.
+For REASSEMBLE failure classification, \emph{precision} asks how many predicted failures were actual failures. \emph{Recall} asks how many of the actual failures were found. Lowering the decision threshold may find more failures while also raising more false alarms. Specificity measures how often successful cases were correctly recognised. Balanced accuracy gives equal weight to recall for the two classes, while the F1 score combines precision and recall for the designated positive class.
 
-Accuracy describes the proportion of correctly classified observations. Precision measures the proportion of positive predictions corresponding to actual positive outcomes. Recall measures the proportion of actual positives identified by the classifier. Specificity measures the proportion of negative observations correctly classified, while balanced accuracy gives equal importance to sensitivity and specificity. F1 combines precision and recall through their harmonic mean.
+The main ranking measure was \textbf{average precision (AP)}. It gives a model credit for placing positive cases high on its list across different thresholds. The study used \texttt{average\_precision\_score}, which weights each precision value by the corresponding increase in recall. Calculating the area under a precision–recall curve by the trapezoidal rule is a different measure. AUROC also measures ranking, but a precision–recall analysis is particularly useful when the event of interest is uncommon \parencite{Saito2015PrecisionRecall}.
 
-Ranking-based measures instead examine how well the model orders positive and negative observations across possible thresholds. The area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, AUROC, evaluates discrimination between positive and negative observations. Average precision summarises the precision–recall relationship and provides a useful measure for classification tasks involving imbalanced outcomes. Precision–recall analysis can reveal aspects of predictive performance that are less apparent from ROC-based interpretation when one outcome class is relatively uncommon \parencite{Saito2015PrecisionRecall}.
+The failure rates in REASSEMBLE and ViFailback show why the positive class must be named. In REASSEMBLE, roughly 11\% of the actions failed. A model that gave every action the same score would have failure AP of about 0.11. In ViFailback, more than 85\% of the eligible episodes failed. The same constant-score rule would already have failure AP above 0.85, despite being unable to distinguish any two episodes. For ViFailback, the study therefore reported AP for failure, AP for success, and the mean of these two values. When both classes are present, that mean is 0.5 for a constant-score model. MSM used abnormal cutting as its positive class. These scores answer different questions across the three datasets.
 
-The metrics available for each study depend on its target, prediction outputs, and completed evaluation protocol. Their inclusion in the shared methodological framework does not imply that every metric was calculated for every experiment.
+Ranking also leaves open whether the probabilities are useful. The \textbf{Brier score} measures the average squared difference between the predicted probability and the recorded binary outcome \parencite{P055}. \textbf{Expected calibration error} groups predictions into probability ranges and compares the scores with the observed outcome frequencies in those ranges. Its value depends partly on how the ranges are chosen and how many cases fall in each. A model can rank failures well and still assign probabilities that are too high. Its probability estimates can also become less reliable when operating conditions change \parencite{P008}.
 
-The principal classification ranking metric is average precision, calculated using the \texttt{average\_precision\_score} implementation. Average precision summarises the precision–recall relationship by weighting precision values by their corresponding increments in recall. This formulation is distinct from estimating the area under a precision–recall curve through trapezoidal numerical integration.
+\subsection{Combining results and estimating uncertainty}
 
-Average precision depends on the prevalence of the designated positive class. If a binary predictor assigns the same score to every observation, its average precision equals the positive-class prevalence in the assessment population. A high failure average precision may provide limited evidence of discrimination when failure already represents the majority of the evaluated observations. This principle is particularly relevant to the ViFailback assessment, where failure constitutes more than 85\% of the principal eligible population. Reporting only failure average precision can therefore obscure the model’s ability to identify successful outcomes.
+For its principal comparison, REASSEMBLE brought together the predictions for all held-out recordings and calculated failure AP once. Calculating AP separately in each fold and averaging the five results would answer a different question, because cases from different folds would never be ranked against one another. MSM calculated pooled abnormal-cutting AP for each training seed and then averaged across seeds. Changing a seed measures variation from fitting a model again. It does not create another independently recorded milling interval.
 
-Where both outcomes are relevant, class-specific average precision can be calculated separately for success and failure. Their binary macro average is obtained by calculating the arithmetic mean of these two class-specific values. For a constant-score binary predictor, the both-class macro average precision reference equals 0.5 when both outcome classes are present. REASSEMBLE principally reports failure average precision. MSM reports abnormal-cutting average precision. ViFailback additionally reports both-class macro average precision. These are distinct evaluation quantities and must not be interpreted as directly comparable numerical measures of architectural performance across the datasets.
+To compare two REASSEMBLE models, the analysis kept their predictions paired. If a recording was selected for a calculation, both models contributed predictions for the same actions in that recording. The core comparison repeated this \textbf{2,000 times}, sampling whole recordings with replacement and recalculating the difference in performance. This is a recording-level \emph{bootstrap}.
 
-The Brier score measures the average squared difference between predicted probabilities and observed binary outcomes. Proper scoring rules provide a principled basis for assessing probability forecasts rather than only their ranking or thresholded decisions \parencite{P055}.
+A PHM report used 10,000 bootstrap samples drawn at the level of the gear run. The data still came from only \textbf{20 runs across three gear experiments}. Runs from the same experiment could share conditions. More bootstrap draws make the numerical estimate more stable, but they cannot supply more independent gear experiments. The MSM date-based intervals had a similar limit: only five acquisition dates were available.
 
-Expected calibration error provides a complementary description based on discrepancies between predicted confidence and observed outcome frequencies across probability bins. Its interpretation depends on the number and construction of bins, the definition of confidence, and the available observations within each bin. A classifier with strong ranking performance may still assign poorly calibrated probabilities. Conversely, a model with improved probability error may not provide better discrimination. Probability calibration can also change under altered input conditions. Evidence from clean-data calibration must therefore be interpreted separately from probability quality under degradation or distribution shift \parencite{P008}.
+The intervals describe variation in the saved predictions under the chosen way of resampling. They do not cover every change that might occur after retraining the models or moving to a new installation. The intervals also leave out uncertainty from model selection and repeated use of the assessment data. Cross-validation folds can also share training observations, which limits what their spread tells us about uncertainty \parencite{Bengio2004CVVariance}. Where an interval for a performance difference included zero, the data left the direction of the advantage unresolved. Showing that two methods perform closely enough for practical use would require a separate criterion for how small a difference counts as acceptable.
 
-\subsection{Aggregation and Uncertainty}
-
-Mean-fold average precision is calculated by obtaining the average precision for each assessment fold and subsequently averaging those values. Pooled average precision instead combines the corresponding held-out prediction vectors and calculates average precision over the combined population. Because average precision is nonlinear, these aggregation procedures generally produce different numerical results.
-
-The original REASSEMBLE primary comparison reports pooled failure average precision across its held-out recording partitions. MSM reports abnormal-cutting average precision averaged over seed-specific pooled predictions. Reported seed variation and fold variation must also remain separate. Different random initialisations or fitting seeds represent another source of experimental variation rather than additional independently acquired assessment observations.
-
-For two prediction models evaluated on the same observations, a paired comparison can be obtained by subtracting the average precision of the reference model from that of the investigated model. The paired construction retains the common observations and corresponding outcomes for the two models. The uncertainty analyses use cluster-based bootstrap procedures where supported by the retained experimental records. A cluster bootstrap resamples the specified dependence groups with replacement, rather than resampling individual observations independently. It then recalculates the paired metric difference for each resampled population.
-
-The core REASSEMBLE reports use 2,000 recording-level bootstrap replicates. A PHM report uses 10,000 run-cluster bootstrap replicates over 20 runs from three gear experiments. The latter resampling procedure requires particular care because runs from the same gear experiment may retain higher-level dependence. Run-level resampling does not automatically capture all dependence between observations originating from the same physical experiment. Increasing the number of bootstrap replicates reduces the numerical variability of the estimated resampling distribution. It does not increase the number of independently acquired physical experiments. Similarly, the MSM date-cluster intervals rely on a particularly limited population of five acquisition dates. Their interpretation remains conditional on those groups and the recorded fitted models.
-
-Cross-validation folds also share training observations. They should therefore not be treated as independent experimental replications when calculating uncertainty. \textcite{Bengio2004CVVariance} establish the limitations of universally unbiased variance estimation for k-fold cross-validation, supporting caution when constructing uncertainty estimates from overlapping training populations. The retained bootstrap intervals characterise uncertainty under the selected resampling unit and fitted prediction vectors. They do not automatically include uncertainty associated with retraining, additional model-selection procedures, repeated assessment exposure, or future industrial installations. An uncertainty interval containing zero does not establish that two methods are equivalent. Likewise, a positive point estimate does not, by itself, establish a practically meaningful or reproducible improvement.
-
-\section{Robustness Evaluation Design}
+\section{Testing missing and damaged inputs}
 \label{sec:ch4-robustness}
 
-Clean-input assessment establishes predictive performance under reference observation conditions. Known modality absence evaluates behaviour when an input is declared unavailable. Sensor corruption and visual degradation examine performance under specified numerical measurement disturbances and changes to image quality, respectively. Temporal misalignment concerns changes in the correspondence between observations, while distribution shift concerns changes in the assessment distribution or operating conditions. Complete input absence requires a defined system response when no predictive branch remains available. The empirical studies test selected conditions from this framework. They do not comprehensively evaluate temporal misalignment, naturally occurring sensing failures, or broader deployment shifts.
+\subsection{What the model knew about the fault}
 
-\subsection{Controlled Interventions, Absence, and Degradation}
+A missing sensor and a faulty sensor can lead to different decisions. In the REASSEMBLE absence test, the evaluator knew when a whole branch was unavailable. When the sensor branch was missing, it returned the camera probability and used the camera branch’s decision threshold. When only the sensors remained, it used their probability and threshold. If both inputs were absent, it rejected the case. Fusion methods that returned the same surviving prediction naturally obtained the same result under this rule.
 
-A robustness experiment requires an explicit description of how the input condition is modified. The relevant intervention should identify the affected modality, the processing stage at which the modification occurs, the severity of the disturbance, and the observations associated with the assessment. When stochastic corruption is used, record the number of realisations and the applicable randomisation procedure. For visual observations, corrupting image pixels before feature extraction represents a different intervention from corrupting a cached visual embedding. For machine-sensor observations, modifying the original channels differs from modifying aggregated statistics or prediction logits. Quality descriptors intended to represent the modified observation must be handled consistently with the intervention. Otherwise, the gate may receive context that does not correspond to the observation actually supplied to the predictor.
+A damaged signal presented a harder problem. In one REASSEMBLE test, six force and torque channels were fixed at their training medians. The sensor branch still received numbers and could still make a prediction. Its statistics and quality measures had to be calculated again from the changed signal. Giving the gate quality measures from the original clean signal would have described a different input from the one the predictor actually received.
 
-The original two-input REASSEMBLE gate assigns the surviving modality a weight of one. Its robustness evaluator directly returns the surviving prediction probability and uses the corresponding threshold. When both inputs are absent, the original implementation rejects the observation rather than generating a prediction. If several fusion mechanisms return the same surviving prediction, their performance under that absence condition is determined by the surviving branch. Such a comparison does not establish that one learned gate recognises modality absence more effectively or provides a better adaptive allocation. Training-time modality dropout can expose the model to selected missing-input patterns, but its effectiveness must be assessed separately.
+For each controlled disturbance, the important details were what changed, where the change was applied, how strong it was, and which observations were assessed. Blurring the camera frames before feature extraction tests a different fault from altering a saved feature vector. For random noise, the number of noise samples and the randomisation procedure also affect the result. HANDOVER, for example, included controlled sensor noise, visual blur, and known loss of an input. ViFailback’s planned corruption evaluation remained unfinished.
 
-A degraded observation can remain available while containing less useful or misleading information. Unlike known modality absence, this condition does not necessarily give the prediction system an explicit indicator of which observation should receive reduced reliance. The relevant scientific comparison evaluates the adaptive mechanism against appropriate static fusion and unimodal references under the same degradation condition. Where one modality remains intact, its prediction provides an important reference for assessing whether fusion with a corrupted companion improves or worsens the resulting decision.
+\subsection{Giving competing models the same exposure}
 
-The motivation for such conditional allocation is supported by quality-aware fusion research, which examines the relationship between dynamic weights, unimodal reliability, and predictive performance under specified assumptions \parencite{Zhang2023QMF}. The empirical investigation must nevertheless establish whether the available descriptors provide useful information about the reliability of the particular industrial or robotic prediction branches.
+A gate trained with noisy signals has already seen a problem that a model trained only on clean signals has never faced. If the gate performs better under noise, the training examples may explain part of the gain. Later REASSEMBLE comparisons therefore gave static and adaptive models comparable exposure to corruption during fitting. This addressed one important difference while leaving others, such as model size and calibration, to be considered. The idea that changing weights can help rests on how well the available information identifies an unreliable input \parencite{Zhang2023QMF}.
 
-\subsection{Matched Exposure and Robustness Interpretation}
+The evaluation considered both the score under damage and the change from the clean score. A model can lose less from its starting point yet still finish below a stronger model. When only the sensors were damaged, the intact camera also provided a useful reference: combining inputs was of limited value if it produced a worse prediction than using that camera alone. The tests support conclusions about the stated faults and their severity. Questions about gradual clock drift, several natural faults at once, or operation in a new factory remain open.
 
-A model exposed to corrupted or incomplete observations during fitting may develop different predictive behaviour from a model trained exclusively on intact observations. If only an adaptive model receives degradation-related augmentation, its performance improvement cannot be attributed exclusively to the adaptive mechanism. Comparisons between static and adaptive models with comparable corruption exposure therefore provide a more informative basis for investigating the contribution of adaptation. Matching exposure addresses one important source of experimental difference. However, differences in model capacity, optimisation procedures, feature representations, and calibration may remain. The subsequent REASSEMBLE anchored-adjustment and quality-interaction investigations retain these distinctions when evaluating clean and degraded predictions. The matched-exposure comparisons address a narrower scientific question than the original comparisons between differently configured static and adaptive fusion models.
-
-Robustness evaluation requires consideration of both absolute degraded-input performance and changes relative to the corresponding clean reference. A model may lose less performance relative to its clean baseline yet still perform worse in absolute terms than another model under degradation. Likewise, an adaptive mechanism may visibly reduce its reliance on a corrupted modality while still performing below the intact unimodal predictor. These findings must also be interpreted against the performance of available unimodal references and appropriately matched static fusion mechanisms. Probability quality and threshold-dependent decisions provide further evidence where evaluated. The robustness findings remain specific to the tested corruption operators, severities, observation windows, and fitting procedures. The completed experiments do not establish comprehensive reliability under unmeasured clock offsets, gradual calibration drift, simultaneous natural sensing faults, or previously unseen industrial installations.
-
-\section{Reproducibility and Experiment Provenance}
+\section{Records, software, and checks on the results}
 \label{sec:ch4-reproducibility}
 
-Supporting records include implementation sources, configurations, preprocessing definitions, dataset manifests, model-fitting procedures, assessment populations, saved predictions, and evaluation artefacts. Accessible code, recorded execution, and independently reproduced experimental findings represent different levels of reproducibility assurance. These distinctions are particularly important in machine-learning research, where model selection, software dependencies, preprocessing, and undocumented configuration changes can affect the reported performance \parencite{P009}.
+A reported score can be checked at several levels. With saved predictions and outcome labels, it is possible to check the sample identities and calculate the score again. Running the fitted model on its inputs also checks whether it produces the same predictions. Training the model again requires the training data, software, settings, and selection procedure. Each step examines a different part of the evidence \parencite{P009}.
 
-The principal REASSEMBLE visual branch loads the pretrained RT-DETR-R18vd checkpoint through the Hugging Face Transformers library and selects its ResNet18-vd convolutional backbone. The Hugging Face Transformers library also supplies the PatchTST sensor encoder, which is initialised from scratch for the investigated prediction task \parencite{Wolf2020Transformers}. PyTorch supports the neural-network implementation and fitting procedures, with AdamW used for the relevant neural prediction components \parencite{Paszke2019PyTorch}. Scikit-learn provides the grouped stratified splitting procedures, logistic regression references, and corresponding evaluation functionality \parencite{Pedregosa2011ScikitLearn}. The PHM visual regression implementation uses the Ultralytics RT-DETR-L prediction path and extracts intermediate feature maps for its downstream regression representation \parencite{UltralyticsRTDETR}.
+The main REASSEMBLE visual branch loaded RT-DETR-R18vd through Hugging Face Transformers and took its ResNet18-vd convolutional backbone. The same library supplied the PatchTST sensor encoder, which was trained from scratch \parencite{Wolf2020Transformers}. The neural models used PyTorch, with AdamW for the relevant fitting steps \parencite{Paszke2019PyTorch}. Scikit-learn supplied grouped stratified splits, logistic regression, and evaluation functions \parencite{Pedregosa2011ScikitLearn}. The separate PHM image model used the Ultralytics RT-DETR-L prediction procedure to collect intermediate image features \parencite{UltralyticsRTDETR}.
 
-\noindent\textit{Internal implementation evidence.} \EvidenceRef{E064}{}, \EvidenceRef{E065}{}, \EvidenceRef{E067}{}, \EvidenceRef{E046}{}, \EvidenceRef{E033}{}, \EvidenceRef{E004}{P01}.
+The manuscript’s source record identifies repository commit \texttt{e32ec43aef69cc58adff0ea91e508308041598c5}. File hashes identify selected source files and reports, including some material held outside that commit. Appendix~\ref{app:reproducibility} links the experiments to the available evidence. Appendix~\ref{app:chronology} records when the work took place. Where an original script or setting was unavailable, the remaining records limit how much of the run can be checked independently.
 
-Source inspection establishes which implementation and recorded computations can be examined. Configuration inspection identifies model settings, preprocessing choices, and fitting conditions. Execution records provide documentary evidence that a particular experimental stage was completed. Saved-prediction verification goes further by recalculating performance measures from retained model outputs. Repeated inference regenerates predictions from the original fitted model and its inputs. Training reproduction fits the model again using the recorded procedure. Independent empirical replication tests the findings with genuinely independent experimental evidence.
+The experimental reporting period extended through \textbf{24 November 2026}. For ViFailback, it included the completed frozen and transferred-feature comparisons, the one-fold \texttt{FULL20} target-backbone comparison, and the \texttt{FULL100} pilot. The remaining target-CNN configurations and the corruption assessment were unfinished at that point.
 
-For historical experiments, the available configuration and execution records identify the documented prediction procedure. Missing production scripts, incomplete historical configurations, or unavailable artefacts limit independent verification.
+On \textbf{25 November 2026}, selected results were checked again from saved predictions. For REASSEMBLE F2, the checks covered sample identities, fold assignments, branch scores, fitted coefficients, and ranking measures. For ViFailback, they covered the available assessment predictions, reported metrics, and records of which stages had finished. These checks establish what could be recovered from the saved outputs. They did not repeat the original neural training or test the models on newly collected observations.
 
-The original manuscript source manifest identifies repository commit \texttt{e32ec43aef69cc58adff0ea91e508308041598c5}. The accompanying provenance records include hashes of selected source files and supporting reports, including relevant material that was not committed within the identified repository version.
